1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,088
Thursday, half past four.

2
00:00:02,138 --> 00:00:07,608
Aziz's phone rings, and a calm voice says that it is the security team of his bank.

3
00:00:07,658 --> 00:00:14,832
Someone is trying to take his money, the voice says, so he must install a small app, right now, to protect his account.

4
00:00:14,882 --> 00:00:17,928
The app lets another person see and control his screen.

5
00:00:17,978 --> 00:00:22,944
Aziz's phone is trusted, his session is valid, and his face is his own.

6
00:00:22,994 --> 00:00:24,816
So what can still go wrong?

7
00:00:24,866 --> 00:00:30,768
In Lesson 24, a new phone proved the SIM card, the old phone approved, and MyID proved the face.

8
00:00:30,818 --> 00:00:37,992
This lesson covers the one danger that none of those checks can see: someone who drives Aziz's own trusted phone.

9
00:00:38,042 --> 00:00:39,864
Look at the checks one by one.

10
00:00:39,914 --> 00:00:41,736
The phone is Trusted.

11
00:00:41,786 --> 00:00:43,320
The grant is valid.

12
00:00:43,370 --> 00:00:48,096
The binding check passes, because every request really comes from Aziz's device.

13
00:00:48,146 --> 00:00:52,272
Even the face is real, because Aziz himself holds the phone.

14
00:00:52,322 --> 00:00:54,960
From the gateway, everything looks perfect.

15
00:00:55,010 --> 00:00:57,504
The danger is not in the request.

16
00:00:57,554 --> 00:00:59,304
It is in the hands that make it.

17
00:00:59,354 --> 00:01:02,400
So the guard must live on the phone, not on the server.

18
00:01:02,450 --> 00:01:04,320
This is the job of the master app.

19
00:01:04,370 --> 00:01:09,816
The mobile bank looks for two things: remote-access software, and an active phone call.

20
00:01:09,866 --> 00:01:11,928
Only the master app does this.

21
00:01:11,978 --> 00:01:15,768
It moves money, so it carries the app self-protection of the bank.

22
00:01:15,818 --> 00:01:20,208
Side apps, like the marketplace, do not detect remote access at all.

23
00:01:20,258 --> 00:01:23,928
The caller's app is now installed, and it starts to share the screen.

24
00:01:23,978 --> 00:01:25,608
The mobile bank finds it.

25
00:01:25,658 --> 00:01:28,728
At once, the mobile bank blocks its own screen.

26
00:01:28,778 --> 00:01:32,592
The caller sees nothing useful, and he can tap nothing.

27
00:01:32,642 --> 00:01:35,136
Then the mobile bank tells the platform.

28
00:01:35,186 --> 00:01:37,224
It sends a remote-access event.

29
00:01:37,274 --> 00:01:42,000
The event carries three things: the device, the time, and the type.

30
00:01:42,050 --> 00:01:45,600
The type is either remote-access software, or a phone call.

31
00:01:45,650 --> 00:01:48,408
Now watch what the platform does, and what it does not do.

32
00:01:48,458 --> 00:01:50,280
It writes an audit event.

33
00:01:50,330 --> 00:01:53,760
It sends an event to the antifraud system of the bank.

34
00:01:53,810 --> 00:01:55,632
And that is all.

35
00:01:55,682 --> 00:01:57,888
The platform does not block Aziz.

36
00:01:57,938 --> 00:02:01,776
It does not change his customer record, his device or his grant.

37
00:02:01,826 --> 00:02:05,088
The phone stays trusted, and his session stays valid.

38
00:02:05,138 --> 00:02:06,936
At first this sounds too soft.

39
00:02:06,986 --> 00:02:10,104
A thief is on the line, so why not lock everything?

40
00:02:10,154 --> 00:02:12,408
I think the soft answer is the right one.

41
00:02:12,458 --> 00:02:14,064
A phone call is not a crime.

42
00:02:14,114 --> 00:02:16,560
Aziz calls his mother every evening.

43
00:02:16,610 --> 00:02:23,256
If every call blocked his account, honest people would be locked out every day, and they would stop trusting the bank.

44
00:02:23,306 --> 00:02:26,112
And a block would punish Aziz for what the caller did.

45
00:02:26,162 --> 00:02:27,984
So the work is split.

46
00:02:28,034 --> 00:02:31,176
The master app protects the screen at the moment of danger.

47
00:02:31,226 --> 00:02:33,288
The platform keeps an honest record.

48
00:02:33,338 --> 00:02:39,960
The antifraud system of the bank sees the pattern over time: the same caller, many customers, the same hour.

49
00:02:40,010 --> 00:02:44,184
In this first version, the antifraud system only receives events.

50
00:02:44,234 --> 00:02:46,632
The platform never waits for its answer.

51
00:02:46,682 --> 00:02:48,504
Back to Aziz.

52
00:02:48,554 --> 00:02:51,672
His screen is dark, and the caller is getting angry.

53
00:02:51,722 --> 00:02:53,544
That anger is a good sign.

54
00:02:53,594 --> 00:02:56,760
Aziz hangs up and calls the number on the back of his card.

55
00:02:56,810 --> 00:03:01,032
The real bank tells him that nobody from the bank asks for an app like that.

56
00:03:01,082 --> 00:03:04,152
This closes the module on devices and login.

57
00:03:04,202 --> 00:03:07,368
Lesson 20 gave the phone a record and a state.

58
00:03:07,418 --> 00:03:10,056
Lesson 21 gave it an access grant.

59
00:03:10,106 --> 00:03:13,104
Lesson 22 showed the way up to level two.

60
00:03:13,154 --> 00:03:19,512
Lessons twenty-three and twenty-four showed the two logins: on a known phone, and on a new one.

61
00:03:19,562 --> 00:03:22,896
And this lesson put a guard on the hands that hold the phone.

62
00:03:22,946 --> 00:03:24,768
To recap.

63
00:03:24,818 --> 00:03:27,648
Only the master app detects remote access.

64
00:03:27,698 --> 00:03:29,256
It blocks its own screen.

65
00:03:29,306 --> 00:03:34,152
The platform records the event and forwards it, and Aziz stays a customer.

66
00:03:34,202 --> 00:03:36,432
But the marketplace has none of this.

67
00:03:36,482 --> 00:03:40,800
It has no MyID, no TAF and no remote-access guard.

68
00:03:40,850 --> 00:03:46,032
So how does Aziz log in to the marketplace on the web, and how does the master app vouch for him there?

69
00:03:46,082 --> 00:03:47,928
Next: side-app login.
